Statement of Work (SoW–DNA-A)
Environmental Microbial Abundance Assessment
Issuing Organization:
 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)
Office of Water – Computational Monitoring Division
Performing Organization:
Academic Computing Laboratory
(ADVCS Parallel Computing Program)
Contract Reference:
EPA-ENV-DNA-2026-A

1. Background & Purpose
The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is conducting a pilot study to assess the baseline microbial composition of environmental water samples collected from a local watershed.
Recent advances in low-cost sequencing enable rapid environmental surveillance; however, scalable computational pipelines are required to process large volumes of sequencing data efficiently.
The EPA seeks a contractor to develop a parallel, reproducible DNA analysis application capable of producing a hierarchical abundance profile from raw sequencing reads.

2. Scope of Work
The contractor shall analyze a single environmental DNA dataset provided in FASTQ format and produce a structured report describing the relative abundance of detected taxa.
The application shall operate on a distributed computing cluster and must conform to the LACC DNA Input Contract.

3. Technical Requirements
The contractor shall:
Ingest one FASTQ dataset from shared storage.
Extract k-mers using a sliding window approach.
Maintain k-mer frequency counts using a custom hash table.
Map k-mers to known organisms using a provided reference.
Aggregate results through a hierarchical taxonomy tree.
Produce a deterministic abundance report.
Parallel execution using MPI (inter-node) and OpenMP (intra-node) is required.

4. Required Data Structures
The solution must utilize:
Feature Mapper: Custom hash table (collision handling required)
Result Aggregator: Tree structure representing biological taxonomy
Top-K Selection: Heap-based priority queue
No external bioinformatics libraries may be used.

5. Deliverables
Executable DNA analysis application
Hierarchical abundance report
Top-K most abundant taxa
Documentation describing algorithmic choices

6. Acceptance Criteria
The solution will be accepted if:
Output is deterministic across runs
Taxonomic aggregation is correct
Performance scales with increased parallel resources
Input contract is strictly followed

7. Educational Alignment (Non-Contractual)
This engagement supports workforce development in parallel computing, data structures, and applied scientific software engineering.




